two weeks after the news of his  intention to resign emerged and amid a  deadlock between sudan's political  parties prime minister abdullah  announced he was stepping down from his  position  despite my efforts to achieve the  necessary consensus to be able to  deliver to our people the promise of  peace justice and no bloodshed that did  not happen during the past few days i  met with all the civilian and military  officials of the transitional council to  explain and put the patriotic and  historic responsibility in front of them  following what i stated i have decided  to return to you what you entrusted me  with and i announced my resignation from  the post of prime minister  abdullah emerged as leader in 2019 after  the overthrow of sudan's authoritarian  president ahmad bashir the country's  main civilian coalition the forces of  freedom and change saw hamdug as the man  to help sudan transition from  dictatorship to democracy  he had previously worked in the ministry  of finance before taking up top jobs in  the african development fund and serving  as the deputy commissioner of the u.n  economic commission for africa  as prime minister hamduk oversaw a  severe financial crisis during which  inflation started beyond 400 percent  but he still managed to end the civil  conflict in various parts of the country  with the juba peace agreement of october  2020  he also successfully lobbied the u.s and  the world bank to remove sudan from the  list of sponsors of terrorism and  convince global financial institutions  to provide debt relief and economic aid  but in october this year the military  led by abdel fattah al-burhan staged a  takeover placing prime minister hamduk  under house arrest and putting members  of his cabinet in prison  a month later hamduk was released and  reinstated after international  condemnation of the coup he then signed  a deal to form a government of  technocrats sidelining political groups  but many sudanese felt he had betrayed  the revolution his removal as far as  they're concerned removes the last fig  leaf that was covering this regime and  what remains is a full-fledged military  military dictatorship so i think the  international community will now uh will  likely increase its pressure on the  military regime uh with the uh hope of  uh heeding the demands of the sudanese  street and and  installing a full civilian rule  demonstrations against the military and  the deal hamdock signed with them have  led to the killings of dozens of  protesters by police and security forces  hamduck has been unable to stop the  violence or install a government that  would remove military interference  and now it seems he is left with no  choice but to concede defeat and resign  hiba morgan al jazeera horton
 

